    "created_at": "2018-04-06T17:25:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-04-06T17:25:40Z",
    "body": "For consistency sake, I imagine `<fieldset>` `form` should follow the same rules as its `disabled` attribute with respect to the child controls: fieldset should override the control.  This to me is what gives this (underused?) element power/usefulness.  In my recent experience, I would have loved to simply wrap a bunch of orphaned controls in a fieldset and set them to its proper form, rather than iterate through each control and apply with JS.\r\n\r\nI have seen a few stackoverflow questions from developers trying to \"undisable\" a control within a disabled fieldset.  So, although it could be useful to allow the child control to have the final say - I wonder if it's good practice?  There are workarounds.  \r\n\r\nMy personal preference and semantic understanding would be that a fieldset's `disabled` and `form` attributes apply to child controls and overrule them.",
