    "created_at": "2022-06-23T12:52:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-06-23T12:52:32Z",
    "body": "I would argue that a `fieldset`'s `form` attribute should **not** override a decendent's `form` attribute. Firstly, such behavior would not be useful. Why would you give an `input` a `form` attribute only to override it then? Secondly, it would violate expectations. The CSS in the following example gives the input a white background, not a red background.\r\n\r\n```HTML\r\n<fieldset style=\"background:red\" form=\"ketchup\">\r\n  <input type=\"text\" style=\"background:white\" form=\"mayo\">\r\n</fieldset>\r\n```\r\nSo associating the input with a \"ketchup\" instead of \"mayo\" form would be against my intuition.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI agree, however, that it would be both helpful for brevity and intuitive, if descendents which have no `form` attribute inherit the nearest ancestor `fieldset`'s `form` attribute. It is confusing that they don't. Evidence:\r\n\r\n- [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/fieldset#attr-form) writes:\r\n  > Please note that usage of this is confusing — if you want the `<input>` elements inside the `<fieldset>` to be associated with the form, you need to use the form attribute directly on those elements.\r\n- Stackoverflow: [Why aren't elements of a fieldset by extension associated with the form the fieldset is associated with?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/49429119/why-arent-elements-of-a-fieldset-by-extension-associated-with-the-form-the-fiel)\r\n- Stackoverflow: [HTML fieldset: form attribute not working](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63009762/html-fieldset-form-attribute-not-working)\r\n- Stackoverflow: [What does the fieldset form attribute really do?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/69432685/what-does-the-fieldset-form-attribute-really-do)",
